# Tutorial 17 — VNS and JES Together
# Launching an entity scene, passing parameters, and returning to narrative flow.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_17_vns_jes
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]

Lavender: VNS and JES are two layers of one project, not competing ways to write the same scene.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Use VNS for dialogue and branching. Use JES when you need entities, components, input, physics, or a scene timeline.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
> When to use each language -> section_roles
> Read the bundled JES scene -> section_scene
> Launch JES from VNS -> section_launch
> Live round-trip demo -> practice_scene
> Quick reference -> quick_reference
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

@label section_roles
[show lavender left talking]
narrator: {b}VNS{/b}: characters, dialogue, choices, variables, menus, and narrative flow.
narrator: {b}JES{/b}: scenes, entities, components, input bindings, timelines, and gameplay systems.
Lavender: Keep story state in VNS unless the entity scene owns it. Pass only the values the JES scene needs.
> Next: inspect the scene -> section_scene
> Back to lesson menu -> lesson_menu

@label section_scene
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Open {b}{{JES_SCENE_PATH}}{/b}. It declares a scene, named entities, components, an Enter-key binding, and a timeline.
Lavender: The file is deliberately small enough to understand in one screen. Change its label text or animation duration and launch it again.
> Next: launch syntax -> section_launch
> Run it now -> practice_scene
> Back to lesson menu -> lesson_menu

@label section_launch
[show lavender right talking]
narrator: The current bridge syntax is:
narrator: {color=#666666}[jes push path/to/scene.jes label return_label with key=value]{/color}
Lavender: {b}push{/b} pauses VNS and puts JES above it. The {b}label{/b} names where VNS resumes. The optional {b}with{/b} values become launch parameters.
Lavender: In JES, invoking the registered {b}return{/b} call pops the scene and resumes VNS.
> Run the live round trip -> practice_scene
> Back to lesson menu -> lesson_menu

@label quick_reference
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: VNS launch: {color=#666666}[jes push {{JES_SCENE_PATH}} label jes_returned with lesson=17]{/color}
narrator: JES input: {color=#666666}on key "ENTER" do return{/color}
narrator: JES structure: {color=#666666}scene → entity → component; timeline for ordered actions{/color}
Lavender: Always provide a reachable return path, and parser-test both files before shipping.
> Run the demo -> practice_scene
> Back to lesson menu -> lesson_menu

@label practice_scene
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Press Enter in the JES scene. VNS will resume at {b}@label jes_returned{/b}.
[jes push {{JES_SCENE_PATH}} label jes_returned with lesson=17 guide=lavender]

@label jes_returned
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Round trip complete. You crossed language boundaries without moving story logic out of VNS.
> Review the lesson -> lesson_menu
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_17]
[flag tutorial_done_17]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_3]
